{
  "item_id": "PRISMA_2020_3",
  "elements": [
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_3_e1",
      "status": "PRESENT",
      "chunk_id": "text_3",
      "quote": "Importantly however, high rates of poor mental health are common in clinical staff working in acute medicine generally [3-5] and so estimating the additional impact 4 Research Department of Behavioural Science and Health, University College London, London, UK of epidemic and pandemic response, not solely the extent of poor mental health, is also important in guiding decisions to protect staff mental health.",
      "reason": "The introduction describes existing knowledge (high rates of poor mental health are common) and the key uncertainty (the additional impact of epidemic/pandemic response beyond baseline)."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_3_e2",
      "status": "PRESENT",
      "chunk_id": "text_3",
      "quote": "Given that the SARS-CoV-2/COVID-19 pandemic is likely to evolve in location, course and severity over time, traditional meta-analyses are only likely to give a snapshot of the current state of the evidence. Living systematic reviews or meta-analyses are a new innovation that allow estimates to be updated as new evidence emerges [7, 8] but have rarely been used to date.",
      "reason": "The manuscript explains why a rapid review/living approach is needed (COVID-19 evolution makes traditional meta-analyses only a snapshot; estimates need updating as new evidence emerges)."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_3_e3",
      "status": "PRESENT",
      "chunk_id": "text_3",
      "quote": "Recently, a meta-analysis by Kisely et al. [6] reported that healthcare workers in direct contact with affected patients had an approximately 1.71-1.74 odds ratio of reporting acute mental health problems compared to healthcare workers not in direct contact with infected patients, and when analysing continuous outcomes, increased scores equivalent to an effect size (standardised mean difference or Hedge's g) of approximately 0.2-0.4. Given that the SARS-CoV-2/COVID-19 pandemic is likely to evolve in location, course and severity over time, traditional meta-analyses are only likely to give a snapshot of the current state of the evidence.",
      "reason": "A prior meta-analysis is cited (Kisely et al.), and the rationale for the current review is explained by noting that traditional meta-analyses provide only a snapshot as the pandemic evolves."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_3_e4",
      "status": "NOT_APPLICABLE",
      "chunk_id": null,
      "quote": null,
      "reason": "The provided evidence does not indicate this is a review of intervention effects; therefore a proposed mechanism of action for an intervention is not applicable based on the available text."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_3_e5",
      "status": "NOT_APPLICABLE",
      "chunk_id": null,
      "quote": null,
      "reason": "The provided evidence describes a rapid review/meta-analysis of mental health outcomes in response to epidemics/pandemics, not a complex intervention with components and outcomes; therefore a logic model is not applicable based on the available text."
    }
  ],
  "confidence": 72
}

{
  "item_id": "PRISMA_2020_4",
  "elements": [
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_4_e1",
      "status": "PRESENT",
      "chunk_id": "text_2",
      "quote": "Purpose The SARS-CoV-2 / COVID-19 pandemic has raised concerns about the potential mental health impact on frontline clinical staff. However, given that poor mental health is common in acute medical staff, we aimed to estimate the additional burden of work involving high exposure to infected patients.",
      "reason": "The abstract provides an explicit objective/question (“we aimed to estimate the additional burden…”) related to the review."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_4_e2",
      "status": "NOT_APPLICABLE",
      "chunk_id": "text_2",
      "quote": "Purpose ... we aimed to estimate the additional burden of work involving high exposure to infected patients.",
      "reason": "The review objective described is about exposure/burden (high vs low exposure to infected patients), not planned comparisons for intervention effects using PICO (or an intervention-effect variant). Therefore, the conditional PICO requirement does not apply based on the retrieved evidence."
    }
  ],
  "confidence": 72
}

{
  "item_id": "PRISMA_2020_5",
  "elements": [
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_5_e1",
      "status": "PARTIAL",
      "chunk_id": "text_4",
      "quote": "Articles were included if they were peer-reviewed articles published in English or Spanish (the languages of the authors) that reported mental health outcomes in clinical staff managing high-risk infectious disease outbreaks. We considered studies on all healthcare staff in all settings as relevant. One article in Chinese with numeric results reported in the English-language abstract was also included. Studies of methodologies including quantitative studies, qualitative studies, and anecdotal accounts, reporting original results were included.",
      "reason": "Eligibility for study characteristics is described (peer-reviewed; language; mental health outcomes; clinical staff managing outbreaks; all settings; quantitative/qualitative/anecdotal; original results). However, follow-up requirements and a full specification of eligible study designs/settings beyond “all settings” are not explicitly detailed."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_5_e2",
      "status": "PARTIAL",
      "chunk_id": "text_4",
      "quote": "Articles were included if they were peer-reviewed articles published in English or Spanish (the languages of the authors) that reported mental health outcomes in clinical staff managing high-risk infectious disease outbreaks. One article in Chinese with numeric results reported in the English-language abstract was also included.",
      "reason": "Dissemination/report language and peer-reviewed publication status are specified, but dissemination year and explicit publication/report status criteria beyond “peer-reviewed” are not reported in the retrieved evidence."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_5_e3",
      "status": "MISSING",
      "chunk_id": null,
      "quote": null,
      "reason": "The retrieved evidence does not state that exclusions were distinguished between cases where outcomes were not measured versus where outcome results were not reported."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_5_e4",
      "status": "PRESENT",
      "chunk_id": "text_4",
      "quote": "We defined high and low exposures as direct contact with infected patients, or work in wards where direct contact was considered highly likely (e.g. critical care, designated treatment wards, emergency departments) compared to clinicians working without direct contact or work in hospital areas where direct contact was unlikely. A narrative synthesis of risk factors was conducted across all studies regardless of methodology.",
      "reason": "The synthesis comparison groups are explicitly defined (high vs low exposure) and the synthesis approach is described, indicating how studies were grouped for comparisons."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_5_e5",
      "status": "PARTIAL",
      "chunk_id": "text_9",
      "quote": "Occasionally, some studies reported two categorisations that would potentially count as 'high risk'-for example, 'working in isolation wards' and 'working directly with infected patients'. In these situations, we chose what seemed like the most likely to reflect 'direct contact' and have opted for transparency through making our decisions clear in the open datasets.",
      "reason": "A rationale is provided for at least one notable eligibility-related restriction/decision (how to classify ambiguous ‘high risk’ categories as ‘direct contact’), but the retrieved evidence does not provide rationales for other notable eligibility restrictions (e.g., inclusion language/peer-reviewed limits)."
    }
  ],
  "confidence": 66
}

